    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I’ve always found math practical, especially in physics and chemistry, which I love. Growing up, my father had no formal education, but my mother, who has a PhD, taught me how to think logically. I use math daily in cooking, budgeting, and even in planning outdoor activities with my kids. It’s not my passion like art or history, but it’s useful and grounding.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Yes, sometimes—especially when the problems get complex or when I’m under pressure, like during school projects or planning a science fair. But I’ve learned to breathe and break things down. My anxiety comes from not knowing the answer, not from the subject itself. I remind myself that mistakes are part of learning, and I’ve had support from my community and hobbies like rock climbing, which helps me stay calm.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I used a simple AI app last year to help with basic algebra when my kids were doing homework. It explained steps clearly and gave me confidence. It wasn’t perfect—sometimes it gave wrong examples—but overall, it made math feel less intimidating. I now use it to check work and explain concepts to my children, which helps build their trust in learning.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, write the equation in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, use the quadratic formula: x = [-b ± √(b² - 4ac)] / (2a). Calculate the discriminant (b² - 4ac) to see if there are real solutions. If positive, two real roots; if zero, one root; if negative, no real roots. Plug in the values and simplify. It’s methodical and works every time.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "Take the derivative of y with respect to x, so f'(x). Set f'(x) = 0 and solve for x. These x-values are candidates for stationary points. Plug them back into the original function to find y-values. Then, check if they are maxima, minima, or points of inflection using the second derivative test. It’s like finding where the slope stops changing.",
    "Briefly, how do you perform a Principal Component Analysis? Should I get anxious about its mathematics? Please, teach me.": "PCA reduces data complexity by finding new axes (principal components) that capture most variance. First, standardize your data. Then, compute the covariance matrix and find its eigenvalues and eigenvectors. Sort them by size and pick the top few components. Project your data onto these. The math is advanced, but you don’t need to memorize it—just understand it as a tool to simplify patterns in data. No need to panic!",
    "According to you, how can LLMs be used to innovate math learning in schools and universities?": "LLMs can offer personalized tutoring, explain concepts in simple language, and provide instant feedback. They can adapt to different learning styles and help students who struggle with traditional methods. In schools, they could support teachers by generating practice problems or helping with lesson planning. With care, they can make math more accessible, especially for students from diverse backgrounds like mine."
